■ Enforcement Decree of the Erosion Control Works Act [Attached Table 1] <Amended 2019. 7. 2.>
Standards, Methods, Projects Subject to Feasibility Assessment, etc. for Erosion Control Works (Related to Article 4-2)

1. Standards for feasibility assessment

| Type of works | Assessment criteria |
| --- | --- |
| Common matters | (a) The site for erosion control works shall be mountainous land or land adjoining mountainous land. (b) Areas planned or confirmed for development for other purposes, or areas where erosion control works are unnecessary, shall be excluded from sites for erosion control works. (c) The site shall not be a habitat of endangered animals or plants. |
| Mountain erosion control works — Landslide prevention works | An area with a high risk of landslides or where landslides are feared. |
| Mountain erosion control works — Landslide restoration works | (a) An area where a landslide has occurred. (b) An area where secondary damage such as collapse, erosion, or outflow of soil and stones is anticipated. |
| Mountain erosion control works — Mountain conservation works | An area at risk of degradation, currently undergoing degradation, or already degraded, where implementing erosion control works would be effective in preventing the collapse or erosion of mountainous land or outflow of soil and stones. |
| Mountain erosion control works — Mountain restoration works | ○ An area at risk of degradation, currently undergoing degradation, or already degraded, where implementing erosion control works can enhance the health, vitality, and stability of forest ecosystems. |
| Coastal erosion control works — Coastal disaster prevention forest establishment works | (a) An area at risk of damage from storm surges, wind waves, drifting sand, salinity, etc., or where damage is occurring or has already occurred, where implementing erosion control works can prevent or reduce such damage. (b) Within 200 meters of the high-tide coastline. |
| Coastal erosion control works — Coastal erosion prevention works | An area at risk of coastal erosion from waves, etc., or where coastal erosion is occurring or has already occurred, where implementing erosion control works can prevent or reduce coastal erosion. |
| Torrent erosion control works — Mountain stream conservation works | An area where erosion of the stream bed or banks is expected, occurring, or has already occurred, where implementing erosion control works can reduce flow velocity and prevent or reduce erosion. |
| Torrent erosion control works — Mountain stream restoration works | An area where damage to a mountain stream is feared, occurring, or has already occurred, where implementing erosion control works can prevent or reduce damage to the stream and enhance the health, vitality, and stability of its ecosystem. |
| Torrent erosion control works — Erosion control dam installation works | An area where damage from upstream landslides or debris flows is feared or occurring, where implementing erosion control works can prevent or reduce damage from landslides or debris flows. |

2. Timing and methods of feasibility assessment
(a) Assessment timing: Assessment shall be conducted after selection of the erosion control works site and before preparation of the detailed implementation design.
(b) Assessors: Assessment shall be conducted by at least three persons with expert knowledge of erosion control works or forest environments.
(c) Assessment method: Assessors shall determine the feasibility of the works according to the feasibility assessment standards for erosion control works, and at least two-thirds of the assessors shall find the works feasible.

3. Projects subject to feasibility assessment
A feasibility assessment shall be conducted for erosion control works falling under any of the following: Provided, That a feasibility assessment may be omitted where erosion control works are implemented to restore damage from landslides or other natural disasters.
(a) Erosion control works with an estimated construction cost of at least 100 million won;
(b) The following erosion control works where the project site area is at least 10,000 square meters:
1) Landslide prevention works;
2) Landslide restoration works;
3) Mountain conservation works;
4) Mountain restoration works;
5) Coastal disaster prevention forest establishment works;
(c) The following erosion control works where the project site length is at least 500 meters:
1) Coastal erosion prevention works;
2) Mountain stream conservation works;
3) Mountain stream restoration works.

4. Other matters
Other matters necessary for feasibility assessments shall be determined and publicly notified by the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service.

■ Enforcement Decree of the Erosion Control Works Act [Attached Table 2] <Amended 2026. 1. 30.>
Management, Inspection, Safety Diagnosis, and Safety Measures for Erosion Control Facilities (Related to Article 12)

1. Management of erosion control facilities
The manager of erosion control facilities shall exercise due care in managing them.

2. Inspection of erosion control facilities
(a) Types of inspection
1) Periodic inspection: Erosion control dams under Article 3, subparagraph 3 (c) of the Act for which four years have elapsed since completion shall be inspected at least once a year: Provided, That inspections may be conducted once every two years as publicly notified by the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service, taking account of the period elapsed since completion, inspection results, etc.
2) Special inspection: Inspections shall be conducted on erosion control facilities where damage from typhoons or heavy rainfall is feared or has occurred and the manager deems inspection necessary.
(b) Inspection methods
1) Visual inspection: Inspect with the naked eye for visible abnormalities in erosion control facilities, including cracks, leaks, or collapse.
2) Detailed inspection: Where the manager deems a detailed inspection necessary based on visual inspection results, inspect for internal cracks, settlement, etc. using nondestructive testing and other methods.
(c) Inspection institutions
Inspections of erosion control facilities shall be commissioned to any of the following institutions [for detailed inspections, only institutions under 1) or 2) qualify]: Provided, That an institution that constructed the relevant erosion control facilities shall not be commissioned.
1) The Korea Forest Disaster Safety Technology Corporation under Article 58 of the Forest Disaster Prevention Act;
2) A public-interest corporation related to erosion control works under the Act on the Establishment and Operation of Public Interest Corporations;
3) A professional engineer office in the forestry field under the Professional Engineers Act;
4) A forestry cooperative or the National Forestry Cooperative Federation under the Forestry Cooperatives Act;
5) An engineering business entity specializing in agriculture and forestry under the Engineering Industry Promotion Act.

3. Safety diagnosis of erosion control facilities
(a) Facilities subject to safety diagnosis
Among erosion control dams installed under Article 3, subparagraph 3 (c) of the Act, those found or suspected to have serious physical or functional defects based on a detailed inspection under subparagraph 2 (b) 2) may undergo safety diagnosis.
(b) Safety diagnosis methods
The safety condition of an erosion control dam shall be professionally diagnosed using safety diagnosis techniques or equipment, including nondestructive testing.
(c) Safety diagnosis institutions
A safety diagnosis shall be commissioned to any of the following institutions:
1) The Korea Forest Conservation Technology Association under Article 22-2 (1) of the Act;
2) An institution conducting detailed safety diagnoses under Article 12 of the Special Act on the Safety Control and Maintenance of Establishments.

4. Safety measures for erosion control facilities
(a) Where inspection under subparagraph 2 or safety diagnosis under subparagraph 3 reveals matters requiring urgent action, emergency measures shall be taken immediately.
(b) Where inspection under subparagraph 2 or safety diagnosis under subparagraph 3 reveals a serious problem affecting the safety of erosion control facilities, necessary safety measures shall be taken, including reinforcement, improvement, demolition, or reconstruction.

5. Other matters
Other matters necessary for the management, inspection, safety diagnosis, and safety measures for erosion control facilities shall be separately determined and publicly notified by the Administrator of the Korea Forest Service.
